# SYNOPSIS

```usage
grep [-EFHchilnqsvwx] [-e pattern] [-f file] [pattern] [file ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`grep` reads each <file>, or standard input if there is none, and prints every line that matches a <pattern>; a <file> of `-` is standard input, named `<stdin>`.
A <pattern> is a basic regular expression, as musl's `regcomp` compiles it, unless `-E` or `-F` says otherwise.
Without `-e` or `-f` the first argument is the pattern; with them it is a <file>.
A pattern that holds newlines is several patterns, one a line, and a line matches if any of them matches it.
With more than one <file>, each line printed is prefixed by its file's name and `:`.

: `-E`
  The patterns are extended regular expressions.
: `-F`
  The patterns are fixed strings, matched as they are.
: `-e` <pattern>
  Use <pattern>; it may be given more than once.
: `-f` <file>
  Use each line of <file> as a pattern; it may be given more than once.
: `-c`
  Print only a count of the matching lines of each <file>.
: `-l`
  Print only the name of each <file> that has a matching line, once.
: `-n`
  Prefix each line printed with its line number and `:`.
: `-q`
  Print nothing; exit 0 at the first matching line.
: `-H`, `-h`
  Prefix each line printed with its file's name, or never, however many <file>s there are.
: `-i`
  Ignore case in matching.
: `-s`
  Do not report a <file> that cannot be opened.
: `-v`
  Select the lines that do not match.
: `-w`
  Match only whole words: the match must have no letter, digit or `_` next to it on either side.
: `-x`
  Match only whole lines.

Of `-c`, `-l`, `-n` and `-q`, the last given wins.

`grep` is sbase's, and found only in the POSIX namespace: `/boot/bin/posix/grep` is a hard link to `sbase-box`, which runs the command its name names, and `/lib/ns/posix` (a manifest's `ns=posix`, see namespace(6)) binds `/boot/bin/posix` before `/boot/bin` on `/bin`, so a POSIX program that runs `grep` finds it there.
There is no native `grep`.

# EXAMPLES

`tests/posix/sbasetest.c` runs `grep ^c` in the POSIX namespace with the lines `cat`, `dog` and `cow` on standard input, and checks that it prints `cat` and `cow` and exits 0.
It runs `grep dog` with `cat` alone, and checks that it prints nothing and exits 1.

